WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.480
In our writing app,

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:03.970
the editor autosaves as people type.

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.300
You ask your coding agent

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.690
to clean up that code.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.290
It finds a sort that looks redundant, removes it,

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.090
and records the change in the model.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.130
The Shape check on the pull request stops it.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:15.810
The team left a note on this function.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.000
The sync library you depend on

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.200
delivers autosaves out of order,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.650
so this merge has to sort them first.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:25.050
It looks redundant, and it isn't.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.930
So any change here needs a recorded review.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.390
A reviewer, a teammate or a review agent,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:32.210
reads the note,

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:34.610
and the agent puts the sort back.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.490
The review is recorded in the model,

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:37.430
with the date.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:38.490
Now it can merge.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:41.970
Your agent can't know every past decision.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.570
Shape keeps them in the model,

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:45.780
and makes sure a review is recorded.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:47.470
Who reviews, and how well,

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.060
is up to your process.
